From 01c7796eee38e8bcfa568a65b9683e68a42cdf6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian Bockelman <bbockelman@morgridge.org>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 07:50:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Require a second factor when the provider did not record
 MFA

Adds an "mfa" section that reads the acr and amr claims the provider returns
from its userinfo endpoint and, when they do not show that multi-factor
authentication was performed, either denies the login or returns
PAM_CRED_INSUFFICIENT so the PAM stack can run a second factor:

    "mfa": {
        "acr_values": ["https://refeds.org/profile/mfa"],
        "amr_values": ["mfa", "otp"],
        "if_absent": "second_factor"
    }

if_absent defaults to "ignore", so existing configurations are unaffected.
Configuring a policy without saying what counts as MFA is rejected rather than
defaulted, since it would otherwise apply to every login.  The check runs after
authorisation, so a user who would be refused anyway is told no rather than
being sent round a second factor first.

The claims come from the userinfo response the module already fetches for the
username and groups, so this costs no extra request and there is no token to
parse or validate.  They are also in the ID token, along with an auth_time that
userinfo does not carry; that was measured and rejected as not worth the JWT
handling.

Identity providers differ over what they report, and four measured behind a
single CILogon client covered four shapes: an acr naming the REFEDS MFA
profile; an acr naming a different context on an account that did use MFA; no
acr but an amr listing the methods; and neither claim.  So the check matches
acr and amr *values* rather than testing whether a claim is present, and copes
with both being absent.  amr is accepted as a bare string as well as the array
OpenID Connect Core specifies, because that is how CILogon sends it for some
providers.

Two consequences worth knowing before deploying it.  A provider cannot be asked
for MFA -- CILogon does not advertise acr_values_supported -- only observed, so
users at providers that perform MFA without reporting it will be sent to the
second factor anyway.  And the claims describe the authentication that started
the provider's SSO session rather than this login, which userinfo gives no way
to bound: enabling MFA on an account and repeating the flow can keep returning
the previous answer until that session ends.

The documented PAM stack uses "cred_insufficient=ignore ignore=ignore".  Both
matter.  With "cred_insufficient=ok" the stack's status is set to a non-success
value that a later success cannot overwrite, so a login whose second factor
succeeded would still be refused.  With a jump for "ignore", accounts under
*bypass* -- root, in the shipped config_template.json -- are carried over the
second factor and onto whatever follows it, authenticating with no credential
at all.  test/pam_stack_test.sh drives both documented stacks with pamtester
and a stub module returning each code the module can return, so neither can
regress unnoticed.

parse_args() caught only json::exception, but Config::load() reports a missing
file and a rejected mfa section as std::runtime_error, and parse_args is called
outside the try block in each pam_sm_* entry point.  Such an exception unwound
out of a PAM module through libpam's C frames to no handler: std::terminate()
and SIGABRT, with nothing logged.  The missing-file case predates this change.

The log messages are written for whoever has to work out why a login was sent
to a second factor: both claims always, with <absent> spelled out, alongside
the values that were configured, and PAM_CRED_INSUFFICIENT announced rather
than returned silently, since a stack that does not handle it produces a bare
login failure that looks exactly like a refusal.

util/oidc-probe/oidc-probe.py runs one device flow and reports which of
userinfo, the ID token, the access token and token introspection carry the
claims, so a site can see what its own providers do before writing a policy.

